{"type":"message","id":"3d2cf6ea","parentId":"c78de58a","timestamp":"2026-05-11T06:20:01.710Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":"{\"id\":\"rs_061f472de89db580016a0174ff302481918156b4e5038e711d\",\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"encrypted_content\":\"REDACTED\",\"summary\":[]}"},{"type":"text","text":"Yes — for **Pi**, the right way is **not** to simulate typing into the terminal input field. Pi exposes a real API for this:\n\n```ts\npi.sendUserMessage(\"message text\", { deliverAs: \"steer\" })\n```\n\nThat sends an actual user message “as if typed by the user” and triggers/queues an agent turn.\n\nImportant details:\n\n- If Pi is **idle**, `pi.sendUserMessage(\"...\")` sends immediately.\n- If Pi is **currently working**, you must specify:\n  - `deliverAs: \"steer\"` — inject after the current tool batch / before next LLM call.\n  - `deliverAs: \"followUp\"` — wait until the agent fully finishes, then send.\n- Pi also has `ctx.ui.setEditorText(...)` / `pasteToEditor(...)`, but that only fills the editor; it does **not** reliably “press Enter”. For automation, use `sendUserMessage`.\n\nFor your use case, the clean architecture is a **Pi extension** that subscribes to the active trail’s SSE stream and calls `pi.sendUserMessage(...)` whenever a new review comment arrives.\n\nSomething like:\n\n```ts\nexport default function (pi) {\n  pi.registerCommand(\"trail-watch\", {\n    description: \"Subscribe to active Entire trail review comments\",\n    handler: async (_args, ctx) => {\n      ctx.ui.notify(\"Watching active trail review comments…\", \"info\")\n\n      // Start background process:\n      // entire trail watch --json\n      // parse JSON lines\n      // on event === \"comment\":\n      //   pi.sendUserMessage(formatComment(event.data), { deliverAs: \"steer\" })\n    },\n  })\n}\n```\n\nSo in Pi you’d run:\n\n```text\n/trail-watch\n```\n\nThen incoming Trail comments become user messages in the **currently ac