{"type":"message","id":"c9371bce","parentId":"9b392a4a","timestamp":"2026-05-11T06:30:44.131Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, I’d make it **on by default** for Pi sessions where an active Trail exists.\n\nSuggested semantics:\n\n- Auto-start on:\n  - `session_start`\n  - `/reload`\n  - maybe after `agent_end` if branch/trail changed\n\n- Command surface:\n  - `/trail-watch` → show status / restart if stopped\n  - `/trail-watch pause` → stop watching until resumed\n  - `/trail-watch resume` → start watching again\n  - `/trail-watch restart` → force re-resolve current trail and reconnect\n\nI’d avoid `stop/off` if the mental model is “this is normally on.” Use **pause/resume**.\n\nState question: should pause survive `/reload`?\n\nI’d recommend:\n\n1. **Default on**\n2. `/trail-watch pause` pauses for the current Pi runtime/session only\n3. `/reload` turns it back on again\n\nThat matches what you said: “when I reload now” it should be on.\n\nIf you want pause to survive reloads, we’d need to persist a custom entry or config. I’d skip that initially.\n\nImplementation detail:\n\n- On `session_start`, call `startWatcher(ctx.cwd, ctx)` after updating footer.\n- If no trail exists, just footer says `no trail for branch`; no noisy error notification.\n- `/trail-watch pause` kills watcher and sets footer/status to `trail watch paused`.\n- `/trail-watch resume` calls `startWatcher`.\n- `/trail-watch` with no args:\n  - if watching: notify `Watching trail #N`\n  - if paused/not watching: start/resume\n\nOne subtlety: the extension currently also updates trail footer on `agent_end`. If the branch changes, we probably want to detect that and restart the watcher on the new active trail. Could do:\n\n- Track `watchedTrail.number`\n- On `agent_end`, resolve current trail\n- If found and different from watched trail and not paused → restart watcher\n\nThat gives “always watching the active trail” behavior.","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"m