{"type":"message","id":"70e6e92b","parentId":"e0130e02","timestamp":"2026-05-11T06:59:17.763Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`loop` is interesting, but I wouldn’t use it.\n\nWhy not:\n\n- As a verb, `loop` describes the implementation pattern, not the user intent.\n- `entire trail loop` doesn’t immediately say “listen for feedback.”\n- It can imply “repeat an operation” or “event loop,” not “subscribe to incoming review comments.”\n- For CLI discoverability, `watch` / `listen` / `follow` are more conventional.\n\nIf we want the command to imply “keep the agent in the review feedback loop,” maybe:\n\n```bash\nentire trail feedback\n```\n\nor\n\n```bash\nentire trail listen\n```\n\nI’d still rank them:\n\n1. **`entire trail listen`** — best short verb for incoming SSE messages.\n2. **`entire trail follow`** — okay, log-tail vibe.\n3. **`entire trail feedback`** — user-facing concept, but less obviously streaming.\n4. **`entire trail loop`** — clever, but ambiguous.\n\nIf your mental model is “agent stays in the loop with reviewers,” I’d maybe use **“listen”** for the CLI primitive and describe it as “keeps the active agent in the review feedback loop” in docs/help.","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_061f472de89db580016a017e40cb388191afe9392f61eab4de\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.5","usage":{"input":468,"output":263,"cacheRead":110592,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":111323,"cost":{"input":0.00234,"output":0.00789,"cacheRead":0.055296,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.065526}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1778482750860,"responseId":"resp_061f472de89db580016a017e3f97788191a6bb9e4eeb4d2f9e"}}
