{"type":"message","id":"2720d46c","parentId":"028643be","timestamp":"2026-05-11T07:00:20.583Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yeah, that’s fair. In CLI/event-stream terminology **“watch”** is very established:\n\n- `kubectl get pods --watch`\n- `watch <cmd>`\n- `gh run watch`\n- `fswatch`\n- “watch for changes”\n\nSo even though it’s broad, users will understand it as “stream updates.”\n\nThe issue isn’t really the verb — it’s the noun scope. `trail watch` sounds like watching the whole Trail. If we keep `watch`, we can narrow the noun:\n\nGood short-ish options:\n\n```bash\nentire trail review-watch\n```\n\nNot great aesthetically.\n\n```bash\nentire trail comments watch\n```\n\nAccurate but longer.\n\n```bash\nentire trail reviews watch\n```\n\nMisleading because “reviews” may mean formal verdicts.\n\nMaybe the best compromise is:\n\n```bash\nentire trail watch\n```\n\nbut make the help and flags very explicit:\n\n```text\nWatch a Trail's code-review comments\n```\n\nAnd treat it as: for now, a Trail’s “watchable live surface” is review comments. Later, if we add more event kinds, the same command can grow naturally.\n\nSo revised view:\n\n- If we want **precision now**: `entire trail comments watch`\n- If we want **short UX and future expansion**: keep `entire trail watch`, but document it as code-review comments today.\n\nGiven your goal is agent-loop automation and the command is hidden-ish/dev-ish, I’d probably keep:\n\n```bash\nentire trail watch\n```\n\nwith help text changed from broad “code review (discussion)” to:\n\n```text\nWatch incoming code-review comments for a Trail\n```\n\nAnd maybe in long help:\n\n```text\nCurrently streams the Trail code-review discussion. It does not watch Trail\nmetadata, commits, checkpoints, or formal review verdicts.\n```","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_061f472de89db580016a017e7cf69481918916a4ca77b941a1\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"open